1
00:00:01,399 --> 00:00:03,669
A fire spirit has set a forest alight.

2
00:00:03,789 --> 00:00:08,398
And two nature spirits, Summer and Winter, are racing to be the one who saves it.

3
00:00:08,849 --> 00:00:16,856
This is Living Forest Duel: a two-player game of recruiting animal spirits, planting trees and fighting fire. Here's how to play.

4
00:00:17,657 --> 00:00:19,317
There are no points in this game.

5
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:25,442
Instead, there are four ways to win, and the first player to reach any one of them wins on the spot.

6
00:00:25,562 --> 00:00:27,800
One: collect eight fire tokens.

7
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,951
Two: fill the recruitment line with nothing but animals of your season.

8
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:35,528
Three: plant nine protective trees in a three-by-three grid.

9
00:00:35,648 --> 00:00:42,133
And four: push the fire spirit, Onibi, all the way to your opponent while they're already holding the Onibi card.

10
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:45,973
Keep those four in mind, because everything you do feeds one of them.

11
00:00:46,773 --> 00:00:48,595
Here's how the table is set up.

12
00:00:49,145 --> 00:00:51,765
It looks busy, so let's take it row by row.

13
00:00:51,884 --> 00:00:59,253
In the middle is the clearing: seven cards in a row. Each one shows a fire value of two, three or four, and a bonus action.

14
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:04,394
Onibi starts on the middle card, and the two cards beside it start with a fire token.

15
00:01:04,515 --> 00:01:07,615
Above the clearing are four piles of protective trees.

16
00:01:07,736 --> 00:01:14,484
Below it is the recruitment line: the stag in the middle, with three animals from each player's own deck on either side.

17
00:01:14,605 --> 00:01:20,534
Then comes an empty space called the shared help line, which fills up during play, and the shared draw pile.

18
00:01:20,653 --> 00:01:26,400
At the front, each player has a starting tree, which is their personal forest, and two action tokens.

19
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:33,441
Each player also gets their own deck of fifteen guardian animals in their season, and seven fire varan cards.

20
00:01:33,561 --> 00:01:38,112
The shared draw pile holds eighteen common animals that belong to nobody.

21
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:47,521
Before you start, if your three cards in the recruitment line cost twelve or less in total, swap the cheapest one for a new card, until the total is more than twelve.

22
00:01:47,971 --> 00:01:53,484
Summer and Winter play exactly the same way. The only difference is which cards are yours.

23
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:58,079
Everything runs on guardian animal cards, so let's read one.

24
00:01:58,629 --> 00:02:03,278
Down the left side are elements. There are four of them, and each one powers an action.

25
00:02:03,397 --> 00:02:09,646
Sun recruits animals. Water puts out fire. Sprouts plant trees. And spirals move Onibi.

26
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:18,968
At the top, some animals show a symbol. A black one means the animal is solitary. A white one means it's gregarious. No symbol means it's neutral.

27
00:02:19,088 --> 00:02:23,576
At the bottom is the season symbol, and, on animals you can recruit, a cost.

28
00:02:24,377 --> 00:02:25,912
Now, how a round works.

29
00:02:26,462 --> 00:02:33,350
Each player has two action tokens. Summer goes first, and in later rounds, whoever didn't play last goes first.

30
00:02:33,471 --> 00:02:39,857
On your go, you do one of two things: reveal the top card of the shared draw pile, or spend an action token.

31
00:02:39,978 --> 00:02:44,754
Revealing doesn't use up a token, so you can keep revealing, card after card.

32
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:49,980
A common animal or the stag goes into the shared help line, where both players can use it.

33
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:58,418
An animal or fire varan of your season goes into your personal line, and only you can use it. One of your opponent's season goes into theirs.

34
00:02:58,868 --> 00:03:05,618
But there's a catch. Every solitary symbol you can see counts against you: the ones in the help line, plus your own line.

35
00:03:05,739 --> 00:03:12,082
The moment you can see a third one, you lose one of your unused action tokens. A fourth costs you another.

36
00:03:12,203 --> 00:03:19,923
Each gregarious symbol cancels one solitary symbol. So the same reveal can cost one player a token, and not the other.

37
00:03:20,043 --> 00:03:24,990
Players take turns like this until both have used up their tokens. That ends the round.

38
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:28,646
Spending a token is the clever part of the game.

39
00:03:29,197 --> 00:03:35,381
You place it so it points at the last element of one type in the help line. Let's say, the last water drop.

40
00:03:35,502 --> 00:03:41,632
Count every water drop from the start of the line up to your token. That total is the strength of your action.

41
00:03:41,752 --> 00:03:45,708
Those drops are now used up, for both players, for the rest of the round.

42
00:03:45,829 --> 00:03:52,620
Then add the water in your own personal line and on your trees. Those are only yours, and you can count them again later.

43
00:03:52,741 --> 00:04:01,964
If a token already points at water, the next count starts after it. And to do the same action twice, you need at least one fresh drop between your two tokens.

44
00:04:02,085 --> 00:04:06,998
So the help line is a shared well, and every action you take drains it for your opponent.

45
00:04:07,799 --> 00:04:09,918
Here's what each element lets you do.

46
00:04:10,468 --> 00:04:16,120
Water puts out fire. Take fire tokens off the clearing, with a total value up to your water.

47
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:25,058
Each token is worth the fire value of the card it sits on. So with five water, you could take a three and a two. Collect eight tokens, and you win.

48
00:04:25,509 --> 00:04:31,330
Sun recruits animals. Take cards from the recruitment line with a total cost up to your sun.

49
00:04:31,451 --> 00:04:38,360
An animal of your season goes into your personal line. Anything else, including the stag, goes to the discard pile.

50
00:04:38,810 --> 00:04:46,149
Then you refill the gaps from your own personal deck. So every time you recruit your opponent's animals, you replace them with yours.

51
00:04:46,269 --> 00:04:52,413
Fill the line with only your season, and you win. The stag is neutral, so it has to be recruited too.

52
00:04:52,863 --> 00:05:01,790
Sprouts plant protective trees. Take one to four trees from the tops of different piles, with a total cost up to your sprouts, and plant them straight away.

53
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:05,843
And spirals move Onibi. We'll come back to that in a moment.

54
00:05:06,644 --> 00:05:10,278
Trees go into your personal forest, and they follow a few rules.

55
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:17,418
Every tree card has a river on it. You must always be able to trace that river back to the lake on your starting tree.

56
00:05:17,538 --> 00:05:23,189
Trees always stand upright. And you can cover an old tree with a new one, but never your starting tree.

57
00:05:23,310 --> 00:05:28,449
Plant a tree next to one with the same bonus action, and you trigger that bonus right away.

58
00:05:28,569 --> 00:05:36,737
Best of all, a tree's elements count towards every action you take, every round. And nine trees in a three-by-three grid wins the game.

59
00:05:37,187 --> 00:05:39,927
Here's what a growing forest looks like in the rulebook.

60
00:05:40,728 --> 00:05:41,887
Now, Onibi.

61
00:05:42,437 --> 00:05:47,108
Spirals move the fire spirit toward your opponent, up to one card per spiral.

62
00:05:47,228 --> 00:05:50,351
Wherever it lands, you get that card's bonus action.

63
00:05:50,471 --> 00:05:54,545
When Onibi runs off the end of the clearing, one of three things happens.

64
00:05:54,665 --> 00:06:01,029
If the Onibi card is still in the supply, your opponent takes it, and Onibi carries on from the far end.

65
00:06:01,149 --> 00:06:05,914
If you're the one holding the Onibi card, you put it back, and Onibi carries on.

66
00:06:06,035 --> 00:06:08,841
And if your opponent is already holding it, you win.

67
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:17,545
Holding the Onibi card hurts, too. Any animal of your season that's revealed goes into the shared help line instead of your personal line.

68
00:06:17,665 --> 00:06:28,052
Some bonus actions give you a Sanki card. Spend it to send a fire varan of your season back as soon as it's revealed, or to use an action token straight after a reveal.

69
00:06:28,853 --> 00:06:32,231
When both players are out of tokens, the fire fights back.

70
00:06:32,781 --> 00:06:37,760
Add up the fire values of every clearing card with Onibi or a fire token on it.

71
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:44,820
Each player compares that with their own water: their personal line, their forest, and whatever is left in the help line.

72
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:56,127
If the fire is strictly higher, you add one fire varan of your season to the discard pile for every burning card. When the discard pile is shuffled back in, those varans return to haunt you.

73
00:06:56,247 --> 00:07:04,927
Then the fire spreads. Each card next to Onibi gets a fire token, if it doesn't have one. At the edge, it spreads to the far end instead.

74
00:07:05,048 --> 00:07:13,399
Finally, everyone takes back their tokens, and both personal lines and the help line go to the discard pile. Then the next round begins.

75
00:07:14,199 --> 00:07:16,360
So what makes Living Forest Duel tick?

76
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:22,721
Every reveal is a gamble. More cards mean more elements, but also more solitary symbols.

77
00:07:22,841 --> 00:07:28,108
Every action drains the shared help line, so taking what you need takes it away from your opponent.

78
00:07:28,228 --> 00:07:36,801
And the four ways to win pull you in different directions: fight the fire, flood the recruitment line, grow your forest, or chase your rival with Onibi.

79
00:07:37,252 --> 00:07:40,738
Watch your opponent, pick your moment, and save the forest first.

